Actually, in the (Real Life) RPGs that I played we did get points for
killing monsters, also for exploring and discovering secrets, for the
cleverness in solving situations, and for acting in character. We had
stats that affected how well to achieve a task, how well we fight and
survive. We had races and roles which both influenced our proficiencies
and possibilities to act. We had the option to fight or flee, to stay
or leave any place. Random events and events triggered by the game
master affected our actually available options or their effectiveness.

It's probably not worth disputing whether NH is a RPG or not, or to
what degree. Besides some inherent differences there's also a lot of
similarities at least.

